:: StoryTitle
WORKING / non-canon — Seal Day

:: Start
Dawn in the scriptorium. Ink still wet on your copying board. You are Verrin, junior scribe — too new for a key, old enough to know which shelf Senior Hale swears by.

A runner from the east court says the smell of smoke is wrong for kitchen fires. Hale does not look up. The utterance scrap, the witness stack, and the empty-pipe manual sit where they always sit: scrap under a weight-stone, stack bound in twine, manual closed with a strip of faded blue.

---

[[Check the east stair|MorningStair]]
[[Stay copying|MorningCopy]]
[[Warn Senior Hale|MorningWarn]]

:: MorningStair
The east stair is cool under your palm. No flame. Only a thin gray that might be mist from the well court.

A clerk hurries past with a covered tray and will not meet your eye. When you return, Hale has moved the witness stack one shelf closer to the door.

---

[[Help Hale with the stack|NoonStack]]
[[Pocket the utterance scrap early|NoonScrap]]
[[Open the rite manual one last time|NoonManual]]

:: MorningCopy
You finish the line. Then another. The rumor becomes a cough in the corridor, then a shout two floors down.

Hale’s hand stops mid-character. “If you are going to run,” he says without looking up, “decide what is worth the weight.”

---

[[Help Hale with the stack|NoonStack]]
[[Pocket the utterance scrap early|NoonScrap]]
[[Open the rite manual one last time|NoonManual]]

:: MorningWarn
“Smoke that is not kitchen,” you say.

Hale sets the pen down. He listens the way he listens to bad chronology — as if the wrong year might correct itself. “Then the east stair first. And you do not touch the southern cage’s copies without me.”

He sends you anyway. The stair is warmer than it should be.

---

[[Help Hale with the stack|NoonStack]]
[[Pocket the utterance scrap early|NoonScrap]]
[[Open the rite manual one last time|NoonManual]]

:: NoonStack
You and Hale take the witness stack between you. Names. Dates. Accusations of who heard what when the Word still answered. The twine cuts your palms.

Halfway to the landing, plaster dust sifts from the ceiling. Hale says, “If the stair goes, leave me. Keep the quarrel alive.”

---

[[Crisis — one armful left|Crisis]]

:: NoonScrap
The scrap is thin: one voice, dated, unsigned. The last attested utterance, or the last anyone dared write down. You slide it under your tunic before Hale turns.

The stack and the manual stay on the table like accusations. Smoke finds the high windows.

---

[[Crisis — one armful left|Crisis]]

:: NoonManual
Gesture, timing, silence where the Word used to sit. No theology — only what still binds. You mouth the sequence once. Your hands remember better than your mouth.

Hale watches. “That book outlives every name in the stack,” he says. “That is not a compliment.”

---

[[Crisis — one armful left|Crisis]]

:: Crisis
The scriptorium is a throat full of heat. The east stair pops. You have one armful — maybe two heartbeats — before the way out is a wish.

On the table, or already in your arms from noon: the utterance scrap, the witness stack, the empty-pipe manual. Choose what leaves with you.

---

[[Save the utterance scrap|CrisisScrap]]
[[Save the witness stack|CrisisWitnesses]]
[[Save the empty-pipe manual|CrisisManual]]

:: CrisisScrap
The scrap rides against your ribs. Behind you the stacks blacken. Hale’s voice is lost in the roar, or he never called.

You make the courtyard. Ash on your tongue. The pipe’s last known breath is still unread.

---

[[Give the scrap to Hale|EndScrapHale]]
[[Hide it in the well|EndScrapWell]]
[[Read it once and burn it yourself|EndScrapBurn]]

:: CrisisWitnesses
Names. Heavy. You stagger. The utterance and the manual stay behind and become the same color as the air.

In the courtyard the stack is still bound. Someone will untie it. Someone will lie about who did.

---

[[Deliver to the southern cage|EndWitCage]]
[[Leave unsigned on the moot bench|EndWitMoot]]
[[Sell a name to a Bedaru buyer|EndWitBedaru]]

:: CrisisManual
You save the method. Voice and names go to ash. The blue strip on the cover has curled to black at the edges.

Years from now, clerks will keep a rite nobody can explain. Tonight you still have to decide who learns the gesture.

---

[[Teach the gesture to one clerk|EndManTeach]]
[[Seal it unread|EndManSeal]]
[[Practice it alone in the ash|EndManAlone]]

:: EndScrapHale
You find Hale by the well court, burned at the sleeve, alive. He takes the scrap without thanks and slides it into a sleeve already dark with soot.

“Now it is a document,” he says. “Not a ghost.” Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndScrapWell
At night you wrap the scrap in oilcloth and lower it behind a loose brick above the waterline. The Academy will say the channel died with the witnesses. You left the breath where the living draw.

Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndScrapBurn
You read it once. The words are ordinary and terrible. Then you feed the scrap to a dying coal so no cage, no moot, no buyer owns the last breath.

Your hands remember the shape of the letters longer than your mind does. Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndWitCage
The southern cage takes custody with clean gloves and a receipt. A clerk does not look at you when they say it: “We index so the quarrel does not become a chant.”

Three accounts stay open. No one chooses. Hale, if he lives, will call it method. You hear piety wearing method’s coat. Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndWitMoot
You leave the stack unsigned on the moot bench before dawn. No receipt. No cage seal. The names become everyone’s problem by breakfast.

Years later a Tyropan gloss will call someone here a prophet — their word, laid on an Emissary-shaped wound. Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndWitBedaru
The Bedaru buyer pays in coin that does not care about Academy years. By week's end a prophecy is circulating that uses one name from the stack and none of the dates.

You bought a road home. The pipe got a story. Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndManTeach
One junior clerk — younger than you — learns the gesture in a rented room that still smells of smoke. You do not explain the Word. You only show what still binds.

The empty pipe has a student. Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndManSeal
You wrap the manual, wax the knot, and mark it with Hale’s shelf-code so it looks official. Unread. Waiting for a century that may never ask the right question.

The method sleeps. Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.

:: EndManAlone
In the ash of the east court you practice until your wrists ache. No witness. No student. The gesture holds or it does not — you will not know which until someone else needs it and you are gone.

Speculative only — nothing sticks to the bible.
